---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import DiagramEmbed from '../../components/DiagramEmbed.astro';

const classAlt = "Six rig types arranged left to right by increasing water depth. A land rig stands on the ground with no water at all. Three bottom-supported offshore types transfer their weight to the seabed: a submersible barge rests its flooded hull on the bottom in under 70 feet, a jack-up lowers three legs to the seabed and lifts its hull clear of the waves in up to 400 feet, and a platform rig sits on a fixed steel jacket in up to 1,500 feet. Two floating types never touch the seabed and hold station instead: a semi-submersible floats on submerged pontoons held by moorings or thrusters in up to 12,000 feet, and a drillship is a ship hull held on dynamic positioning in up to 15,000 feet. The dividing line is whether the rig rests on the bottom or floats, and that is what the water depth limit is really measuring.";

// FAQ pairs are rendered as visible content below as well as structured data.
// Marking up questions that are not on the page is a structured data violation.
const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What are the main types of drilling rig?',
    a: 'Land rigs drill onshore. Offshore, the types divide by how the rig is held in place: submersible barges, jack-ups and platform rigs rest their weight on the seabed, while semi-submersibles and drillships float and hold station on moorings or thrusters. That one distinction — bottom-supported or floating — is what sets each type\u2019s water depth limit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a conventional drilling rig?',
    a: 'It depends on who is speaking. To a rig mover, a conventional rig is one dismantled into loads and rebuilt at the next location, as opposed to a mobile, telescoping or walking rig. To a driller, conventional means the string is turned by a kelly and rotary table rather than a top drive. To a geologist, conventional refers to the reservoir rather than the rig. Ask which is meant before answering.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between a jack-up and a semi-submersible?',
    a: 'A jack-up lowers legs to the seabed and lifts its hull above the waves, so it is a fixed structure while it works — cheaper and very stable, but limited to roughly 400 feet of water and dependent on seabed that will carry the legs. A semi-submersible floats on submerged pontoons and holds station on moorings or thrusters, which costs more but works in thousands of feet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which rig type works in the deepest water?',
    a: 'Drillships, at up to about 15,000 feet of water, with semi-submersibles close behind at around 12,000. Both are floaters, which is the reason: once a rig stops resting on the seabed, water depth stops being a structural limit and becomes a station-keeping and riser problem instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What decides which rig is used on a well?',
    a: 'Water depth rules out most options first. Seabed conditions can eliminate a jack-up even in shallow water if the ground will not carry a spudcan. Weather and station-keeping decide between moored and dynamically positioned floaters. The well programme decides the rest — one well over an existing platform is a different job from a multi-well exploration campaign.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a workover rig a drilling rig?',
    a: 'No. A workover or pulling unit services wells that already exist — pulling tubing and rods, changing downhole equipment — and is far smaller than a drilling rig. Coiled tubing and snubbing units are also well service rather than drilling, and snubbing units are built to work on a live well under pressure, which a drilling rig is not.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
---
<BaseLayout
  {/* Search Console shows ~261 impressions/month across "types of drilling
      rigs" and its two dozen variants, at position 79-97. The old title ran 63
      characters, becoming 76 once BaseLayout appends " | RigFloorHQ", so it was
      cut mid-list. This is 44, landing at 57. */}
  title="Types of Drilling Rigs and What Decides Each"
  description="The six drilling rig types compared — land, barge, jack-up, platform, semi-sub and drillship — what separates them and what decides which drills a well."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Equipment', href: '/equipment' }, { label: 'Rig Types', href: '/equipment/rig-types' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/equipment/rig-types"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/equipment/rig-types" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Equipment — Rig Classification</div>
    <h1>Types of <span>Drilling Rigs</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Different environments require fundamentally different rig designs. Water depth, geological conditions, and economics determine which type is deployed.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Classification</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">One Question Sorts <span>Every Rig</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;max-width:780px;margin-bottom:14px;">Rig types look like a list to memorise. They are not. Almost every difference between them follows from a single question: <strong style="color:var(--white);">does the rig rest on the bottom, or does it float?</strong></p>
    <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;max-width:780px;margin-bottom:14px;">A rig that rests on the bottom is limited by how far down the bottom is. Legs, jackets and hulls can only reach so far, which is why bottom-supported types stop at hundreds of feet rather than thousands. A rig that floats has no such limit — but it now has to <em>stay</em> over the well, so its problems become station-keeping, riser length and the hydrostatic head of a mile of seawater. The water depth figures below are really measuring which of those two problems the rig was built to solve.</p>

    {/* Two layouts, not one scaled down. The desktop diagram is 1200 wide, so
        on a phone its labels render at roughly a third size and stop being
        readable — and this audience is overwhelmingly on a phone. The mobile
        version stacks the same six rigs vertically at 420 wide so the type
        stays legible. <picture> fetches only the matching one, and both carry
        width/height so neither shifts layout while loading. */}
    <picture>
      <source media="(max-width: 700px)" srcset="/images/blog/rig-types-classification-mobile.svg" width="420" height="820" />
      <img src="/images/blog/rig-types-classification.svg" alt={classAlt} width="1200" height="470" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;margin:26px 0;border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:6px;" />
    </picture>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    {/* This heading was previously sr-only, added to fix an h1 -> h3 skip.
        It is visible now that the section has a real title, and the hidden one
        is gone rather than left alongside it — two headings for one section is
        worse than the skip was. */}
    <div class="section-eyebrow">The Types</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">The Six <span>Rig Types</span></h2>
    <div class="rig-types grid-auto-300">
      <div class="rig-card" style="border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:3px;padding:24px;background:var(--ink);position:relative;overflow:hidden;">
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:20px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:6px;">Land Rig</h3>
        <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;display:block;margin-bottom:12px;">Onshore</span>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:12px;">Truck-mounted or skid-mounted rigs that drill on land. Range from shallow exploration wells to deep shale plays. The most common rig type worldwide.</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;">
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Depth capacity</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">500 – 35,000 ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Mob cost</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">$10K – $500K</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rig-card" style="border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:3px;padding:24px;background:var(--ink);">
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:20px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:6px;">Jack-up Rig</h3>
        <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;display:block;margin-bottom:12px;">Shallow Offshore</span>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:12px;">Three or four retractable steel legs punch into the seabed, jacking the hull above wave height. The workhorse of shallow-water drilling.</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;">
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Depth capacity</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">up to 35,000 ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Water depth</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">≤ 400 ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Day rate</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">$80K – $180K/day</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rig-card" style="border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:3px;padding:24px;background:var(--ink);">
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:20px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:6px;">Semi-submersible</h3>
        <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;display:block;margin-bottom:12px;">Deepwater</span>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:12px;">Pontoons flooded with seawater provide stability below the wave zone. Anchored or dynamically positioned. Used in deepwater plays.</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;">
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Depth capacity</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">up to 40,000 ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Water depth</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">up to 12,000 ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Day rate</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">$250K – $500K/day</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rig-card" style="border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:3px;padding:24px;background:var(--ink);">
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:20px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:6px;">Drillship</h3>
        <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;display:block;margin-bottom:12px;">Ultra-deepwater</span>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:12px;">A purpose-built ship with a moon pool for the drill string. Dynamic positioning keeps it on location without anchors.</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;">
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Depth capacity</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">40,000+ ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Water depth</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">up to 15,000 ft</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rig-card" style="border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:3px;padding:24px;background:var(--ink);">
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:20px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:6px;">Platform Rig</h3>
        <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;display:block;margin-bottom:12px;">Fixed / Production</span>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:12px;">Fixed to the seabed via steel jacket or concrete gravity base. Permanently installed, drilling dozens of wells from one location.</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;">
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Water depth</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">up to 1,500 ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Well capacity</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">20 – 60+ wells</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rig-card" style="border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:3px;padding:24px;background:var(--ink);">
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:20px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);margin-bottom:6px;">Submersible / Barge</h3>
        <span style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;display:block;margin-bottom:12px;">Swamp / Shallow</span>
        <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);line-height:1.65;margin-bottom:12px;">Flooded hull rests on the seabed in very shallow water — bays, swamps, and marshes. Common along the Gulf Coast and Southeast Asia.</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;">
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Water depth</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">≤ 70 ft</span></div>
          <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;font-size:12px;"><span style="color:var(--fog);">Terrain</span><span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--mist);">Marsh, bay, lake</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Comparison</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Jack-Up vs <span>Semi-Submersible</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:640px;margin-bottom:24px;">The two most common offshore rig types are often compared directly since they can overlap in shallower water depths.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Jack-up Rig</th><th>Semi-Submersible</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Water Depth</td><td>Up to ~400 ft</td><td>400 – 12,000 ft</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stability Method</td><td>Legs rest on seabed</td><td>Floats, held by anchors or DP</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mobility</td><td>Towed, legs must jack down/up</td><td>Can reposition more easily</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Day Rate</td><td>Lower ($80K–$180K)</td><td>Higher ($250K–$500K)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Shallow shelf drilling</td><td>Deepwater exploration & development</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Selection</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">What Actually Decides <span>the Rig</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:740px;margin-bottom:24px;">Rig selection is not a preference. Four constraints narrow it to one or two realistic options before anyone considers cost.</p>

    <div class="card-grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Water Depth</h3>
        <p>The hard limit. A jack-up cannot stand up in water deeper than its legs, which caps it around 400&nbsp;ft. Past that the rig has to float, and a floater has to hold station instead of resting on the seabed — which changes the whole design, not just the hull.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Seabed Conditions</h3>
        <p>Jack-ups need seabed that will carry a spudcan without punch-through — a sudden leg penetration into a soft layer under a stiff crust. Site surveys exist largely to find this before the rig does. Soft or uneven seabed can rule out a jack-up in water shallow enough for one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Weather and Station-Keeping</h3>
        <p>A moored semi-sub is good in heavy seas but slow to relocate. A dynamically positioned drillship holds position on thrusters, moves quickly between locations, and is preferred for exploration — but it depends on power and DP systems staying up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Well Programme</h3>
        <p>One development well over a fixed platform is a different job from a multi-well exploration campaign across a licence block. Platform rigs and tender-assisted rigs exist because moving a full floating rig for routine work over an existing structure is not economic.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note-box mt-24" style="max-width:800px;">
      <strong>Day rate is not the cost.</strong> The figures above are what the rig contractor charges. Total well cost includes spread rate — the rig plus every service company, vessel and helicopter running alongside it — which typically dwarfs the rig line item. A cheaper rig that takes longer, or that cannot work in the prevailing weather window, is not cheaper.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Onshore</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Land Rigs Are <span>Not One Thing</span></h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;">Most rigs working worldwide are on land, and they vary as much as offshore units do. The dividing line is mobility: how fast the rig can be broken down, moved and stood back up.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Conventional</strong> rigs are dismantled into loads and rebuilt at the next location, which can take days. <strong style="color:var(--white);">Mobile</strong> and telescoping-mast rigs are designed to rig down and move far faster.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;"><strong style="color:var(--white);">Walking</strong> and skidding rigs go further: they lift and step themselves between wells on a multi-well pad without rigging down at all, which is why they dominate unconventional pad drilling where twenty-plus wells share one location.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;">That capability is what makes long-reach horizontal programmes economic, and it ties directly to <a href="/drilling/directional" style="color:var(--amber);">directional drilling</a> — the rig stays put while the wells fan out underground.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col mt-24">
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Why the mobility class matters more than the horsepower</h3>
        <p>On a pad programme, a rig that walks 40 feet to the next conductor in a shift is doing something a conventional rig would need days to do. Across twenty wells that difference is not a convenience, it is the economics of the whole pad. Rig-up and rig-down time is dead time paid at spread rate, and spread rate is the number that decides whether a well makes money.</p>
        <p>It is also why comparing land rigs by depth rating alone is misleading. Two rigs rated to the same depth can differ by a factor of ten in how long they take to get from one well to the next.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Terminology</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">What <span>"Conventional"</span> Actually Means</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>This is the most-confused word on the subject, because three different trades use it for three different things and none of them is wrong.</p>
        <p><strong>To a rig mover</strong>, a conventional rig is one that has to be dismantled into loads and rebuilt at the next location — the opposite of mobile, telescoping, walking or skidding. This is the sense used in the section above.</p>
        <p><strong>To a driller</strong>, conventional means the string is turned from the floor by a kelly and rotary table, rather than by a top drive hanging in the mast. That distinction is real and consequential: a top drive rotates while tripping and drills in 90-foot stands instead of 30-foot singles, which changes connection count, stuck-pipe response and how quickly the string can be reamed out of trouble.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><strong>To a geologist or a commercial team</strong>, conventional describes the reservoir, not the rig — a permeable formation that flows without stimulation, as opposed to unconventional shale that needs hydraulic fracturing. A perfectly modern walking rig with a top drive can be drilling an unconventional play, and frequently is.</p>
        <p>The practical advice is short: when someone says conventional, establish which of the three they mean before agreeing with them. On a bid, in a scope of work, or in a handover, the three are not interchangeable and assuming the wrong one changes what you think you are getting.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Adjacent</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Rigs That Are <span>Not Drilling Rigs</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:740px;margin-bottom:24px;">Plenty of things on a lease get called a rig. Most of them do not drill, and the distinction matters when you are reading a job advert or a daily report.</p>
    <div class="card-grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Workover / Pulling Unit</h3>
        <p>Services wells that already exist — pulling tubing and rods, changing downhole pumps, replacing packers. Much smaller than a drilling rig, often a single truck-mounted mast, and it moves between wells in hours rather than days.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Coiled Tubing Unit</h3>
        <p>Runs one continuous reel of tubing rather than jointed pipe, so there are no connections to make. Used for clean-outs, stimulation and fishing, and it can work on a live well without killing it first.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Snubbing Unit</h3>
        <p>Forces pipe into a well that is under pressure, working against the well pushing back. Built for exactly the situation a drilling rig is not: an live, unkilled wellbore. Also the tool of last resort in some <a href="/pillars/stuck-pipe-and-fishing-operations" style="color:var(--amber);">fishing operations</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Tender-Assisted Rig</h3>
        <p>A genuine drilling rig, but split in two: a compact drilling package sits on the platform while a tender vessel alongside carries the mud, power and accommodation. Used where a full platform rig would be too heavy or too expensive for the structure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}

        <h2 id="reuse">Reuse the diagram</h2>
        <DiagramEmbed
          src="/images/blog/rig-types-classification.svg"
          alt={classAlt}
          title="The six rig types"
          pagePath="/equipment/rig-types"
          lang="en"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="card" style="border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:4px;">
          <h3>Related Reading</h3>
          <a href="/equipment/bop" class="card-link">→ BOP Types &amp; Stack</a><br><br>
          <a href="/equipment/drill-string" class="card-link">→ The Drill String</a><br><br>
          <a href="/drilling/directional" class="card-link">→ Directional Drilling</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
